// ---------- the Money from London page ----------
I.london = {
 lead: 'Queensland’s runs were stocked and fenced with borrowed money. Some of it came from banks and companies based in London and Scotland, or from British savers who put money into them. This page follows that money from the 1860s to the Federation Drought, and says plainly what the sources do and do not show.',
 how: [
  'Each company and bank on the list for this page was checked against sources for its actual part in Queensland. Some were barely in Queensland before the 1890s. Those findings are on this page too, so nobody has to guess.',
  'Many of the best sources are histories of all of Australia. Where a figure is not for Queensland alone, the text says so.',
  'Some source pages blocked automated fetching or timed out. Where that happened the source note says the page was read as a search-tool excerpt. Those facts need a check against the full page.',
  'No source read says what London investors knew about how the land had been taken. That gap is listed at the end of the page.'
 ],
 sections: [
  { h: 'Why a run needed borrowed money', p: [
   'A person could not lease a run until it was stocked. Wallis and Burke write that, in the Burke district, people who wanted a run were obliged to stock the land before they could apply to lease it from the government. Stock, fencing and wages had to be paid for before the first wool was sold.',
   'The Queensland Government’s own review of 1909 says it was hard to get produce to port from remote stations. It says one wool clip was often kept on a station until the next year’s shearing had begun. So a squatter usually had to give a lien on the clip. A lien is a claim on the wool, so the lender is paid from it first. The review says the interest rate, including the agent’s commission, was commonly 12 per cent a year.',
   'The Liens on Wool Act was one of the laws of the first session of the Queensland Parliament, which closed in September 1860. The 1909 review says it also covered mortgages on sheep, cattle and horses. A stock mortgage lets a lender claim the animals if the loan is not repaid.'
  ], src: ['atfBurke', 'ofhc'] },
  { h: 'The agency houses and pastoral companies', p: [
   'An agency house, or stock and station agent, sold a squatter’s wool and supplied goods. Many also lent money. The Australian Dictionary of Biography says Frederick Dalgety built a London-linked business that gave wool-growers finance, transport, storage, insurance, technical advice and wool sales. It says that Britain provided both the market and the long-term capital.',
   'Merrett explains the wider system for all of Australia. Banks lent to specialist companies such as pastoral finance companies. Those companies also took deposits and sold debentures, which are written promises to repay a loan with interest. They lent that money on to the pastoral industry.',
   'Merrett quotes an estimate from the Australasian Insurance and Banking Record. It says British investors had put £18 million into the shares of pastoral companies and £28 million into their debentures, and that British insurance companies had lent another £7 million on mortgages over squatting properties. These figures are for all of Australia, not Queensland alone, and they are an estimate from a journal of the time.'
  ], src: ['adbDalgety', 'merrett'] },
  { h: 'Owners who lived somewhere else', p: [
   'Peter Woodley writes that by 1890 Queensland’s properties were enormous. The largest ran 375,000 sheep, and they were generally owned by pastoral and finance companies. He says the owners of these large enterprises often lived elsewhere.',
   'He writes that many of the entrepreneurs with Queensland pastoral interests had Melbourne connections, because since the 1860s Melbourne had been home to banks and pastoral companies with assets in the northern colony. He names the Fairbairn family as an example. George Fairbairn the younger managed several Queensland stations and became general manager of the family’s businesses and managing director of the United Mortgage and Agency Company of Australia Limited. Woodley says the historian Stephen Svensen estimates that the Fairbairns owned an interest in one sheep in every five in Queensland.',
   'The Queensland Historical Atlas says much of the expansion of the 1860s came largely from Victorian wealth invested in Queensland runs. Wallis and Burke say the first pastoralists in the Burke district were often backed by finance from the big cities. They say David McConnel’s Cressbrook, taken up in the Brisbane Valley in 1841, had substantial family funding from England, and that interest in the Warrego became apparent in the 1870s when parties from Melbourne began investing.'
  ], src: ['woodley', 'qhPast', 'atfBurke', 'atfMoreton', 'atfWarrego'] },
  { h: 'Case study: Bowen Downs and the Scottish Australian Investment Company', p: [
   'William Landsborough and Nat Buchanan found the country at Bowen Downs. The James Cook University archive says the Scottish-Australian Company provided the capital for the station. Wallis and Burke say the station was on the lands of the Iningai people and that its first cattle arrived in October 1862.',
   'The local history says the partners took up the country as the Landsborough River Company. It says the Scottish Australian Company, represented by Morehead and Young, held half the shares, Landsborough a quarter, and Nat Buchanan and Edward Cornish an eighth each. Wikipedia says that after a drought Buchanan left the station in 1867 and gave his eighth share to Morehead.',
   'The local history says Bowen Downs eventually covered 3,683 square miles. It says the company set up Mt Cornish as a separate cattle station in 1872 and sold it in 1904 to recover some of its losses. The records at James Cook University include wage ledgers for shearers and other men from 1872.',
   'Wallis and Burke also say the Landsborough River Company, including Morehead and Young of the Scottish Australian Investment Company, had stocked Beamesbrook, near Burketown in the Burke district, by 1865.'
  ], src: ['jcuBowen', 'atfMitchell', 'muttaburra', 'wikiBowen', 'atfBurke'], flag: 'Sources disagree about the first manager of Bowen Downs. The James Cook University archive and Wikipedia say Nat Buchanan. Wallis and Burke say Alexander “Long” Gordon. They also disagree on its early stock and size: Wikipedia gives 3,000 cattle and 1,500 square miles, and the local history gives 5,000 cattle in October 1862, with more cattle and 25,000 sheep added soon after.', image: 'bowenSheds' },
  { h: 'When the lender took the run', p: [
   'The best-documented example from the 1860s is John Douglas, who later became Premier. Jeremy Hodes writes that Douglas bought Tooloombah station in 1860 and had to take out a £5,000 mortgage with the Sydney firm Gilchrist, Watt and Company to pay for it. The panic of 1866 hurt his finances. In 1867 he was forced to transfer Tooloombah to the firm, which financed its purchase by the Beardmore brothers the next year. The sale did not fully clear his debt, and he was declared insolvent in 1872.',
   'Hodes quotes a squatter who wrote that the panic of 1866 “played the mischief with all pastoralists who were either largely in debt or whose credit was not good”. He also quotes James Taylor, a squatter and member of parliament, saying in 1868 that “Ninety-nine out of every hundred squatters in Queensland were insolvent.” That was a politician’s statement in a debate, not a count.',
   'Wallis and Burke give another case. In the Kennedy district the run Inkerman was taken over by Robert Towns by mortgage in 1864.',
   'In the 1890s the same thing happened on a larger scale. The Queensland Historical Atlas says the Australian Mercantile Land and Finance Company held 80 stations as security for loans by 1904, after taking foreclosed land in the 1890s. Six of them were in Queensland. To foreclose is to take the property because a loan was not repaid. The National Museum of Australia says banks foreclosed on some stations in the Federation Drought. The Queensland National Bank held pastoral stations too. The Queensland National Pastoral Company was set up in 1915 to acquire its station interests.'
  ], src: ['hodesDouglas', 'atfKennedy', 'qhAMLF', 'nma', 'eoasQNPC'] },
  { h: 'The 1866 financial crisis', p: [
   'Hodes sets out what happened. From separation in 1859 Queensland governments spent more than they earned, mostly on railways. In 1864 parliament approved a loan of over £1 million and asked the Union Bank of Australia to place the debentures. The bank placed only £300,000 worth by the end of 1865, and it had made large advances to the Government against the unsold bonds. In May 1866 a new loan of £1,170,950 was authorised. The Sydney agents of the Agra and Masterman’s Bank offered to place £500,000 of it in London, and the Union Bank agreed to advance another £100,000.',
   'The London firm Overend, Gurney and Company failed. Wikipedia says it closed on 10 May 1866 and that Agra and Masterman’s Bank suspended payment on 6 June 1866 after the panic that began then. Hodes writes that the news reached Brisbane on 10 July 1866. The Government was told that the new loan could not go ahead. The Union Bank treated its extra £100,000 as cancelled and disallowed government cheques.',
   'Wikipedia says the Bank of Queensland, which was set up in London and opened in Brisbane in 1863, suspended payment. Its London board said a large part of its capital had been lost on advances against sheep and cattle stations, sawmills and even newspaper proprietors.',
   'The Premier, Arthur Macalister, said the Government would issue unsecured government notes. Governor Bowen refused to sanction them, and Macalister’s ministry resigned on 18 July. A bill for £300,000 of Treasury bills at 10 per cent interest passed on 20 July. Hodes says that on 22 July a telegram said Agra and Masterman’s could now carry out its agreement, and that local banks and the Treasury bills eased the crisis.',
   'Even so, public works stopped, including the railway from Ipswich to Toowoomba and the new Parliament House. There were 156 bankruptcies that year. Immigration stopped and unemployed workers left for the southern colonies. Hodes says the recession ended after gold was found at Gympie late in 1867. He adds that for the pastoral industry, already hurt by drought, prices and profits did not recover until the early 1870s.'
  ], src: ['hodes1866', 'agraWiki', 'boq'], flag: 'The sources differ on the details. Wikipedia’s page on Agra and Masterman’s says it suspended on 6 June 1866, but Hodes says the bank was “involved in” the failure of Overend Gurney and that it sent a telegram on 22 July saying it could carry out the agreement after all. Wikipedia’s page on the Bank of Queensland puts the Overend Gurney collapse in July 1866, but its page on Agra and Masterman’s says 10 May. This resource uses 10 May for the London failure and 10 July for the news in Brisbane.' },
  { h: 'The 1880s land boom', p: [
   'The 1909 Government review describes the 1880s. It says money from outside Queensland came in through financial institutions at lower rates of interest. It says prices in many cases doubled and quadrupled in a few months “without much regard to the income-earning power”, and that demand for real property almost stopped in the second half of 1888. It adds that from then until 1893 values were as far as possible held up by the mortgagees, because they believed the stagnation must be temporary. A mortgagee is a lender who holds a mortgage.',
   'Merrett’s figures show how much lending grew. In Queensland, bank advances were 35.6 per cent of the colony’s output in 1880 and 91.6 per cent in 1892. He says Queensland’s output peaked in 1888 and bank advances peaked in 1889. Woodley writes that in the 1880s the number of sheep in Queensland more than doubled, and the Queensland Historical Atlas says sheep numbers peaked at 21 million by 1892.',
   'Some companies were new in Queensland in these years. The New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company opened branches in Brisbane and Rockhampton in 1884. Dalgety and Company Limited was registered in London that year, but its first Queensland branch, at Rockhampton, opened only in 1891. The Muttaburra local history says the first large land resumptions at Bowen Downs were in 1885 to 1887, in a decade of droughts and falling wool prices.'
  ], src: ['ofhc', 'merrett', 'woodley', 'qhPast', 'anuNZLM', 'anuDalgety', 'muttaburra'] },
  { h: 'The 1893 bank failures', p: [
   'The RBA says the Queensland National Bank, the Bank of North Queensland and the Royal Bank of Queensland suspended payment within three days of each other in mid May 1893. Together they held just over half of Queensland’s banking assets. To suspend payment means to stop paying depositors on demand.',
   'Scottish money mattered. The RBA says a run by Scottish depositors in May 1893 prompted the Queensland National Bank to suspend. Merrett says British deposits were about 40 per cent of the total in Australian banks, and that much of them matured at the end of the Scottish Whitsun term in mid May. The RBA also says that in April 1892 the proceeds of Queensland Government loans raised in London were used to meet depositors’ withdrawals from the same bank.',
   'The bank had close links with the Government. The RBA says Thomas McIlwraith was on its board for five years before he became Premier in 1879, and that the bank was awarded all the Queensland Government’s business that year. The Australian Dictionary of Biography says its general manager, Edward Drury, lent £328,000 to McIlwraith and £67,000 to himself.',
   'The Government agreed to a reconstruction after its auditor reported that the bank was solvent. Under the reconstruction, £2 million of the Government’s own deposits was locked up for between 6 and 12 years. The RBA says the bank’s losses were hidden by accounting fraud in 1894 and 1895. In 1897 a government-appointed committee found the bank deeply insolvent. The Government then guaranteed all its deposits for a year. The RBA says the Government was fully repaid in 1918.',
   'Other firms were hurt. The New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company suspended business in July 1893 and was reconstructed in 1894. Burns Philp lost two of its three banks, the Queensland National Bank and the Bank of North Queensland. Merrett says that if the whole system had failed, only a handful of banks might have survived, with the Bank of New South Wales, the Union Bank of Australia and the Bank of Australasia as the contenders.',
   'Merrett says Queensland’s output peaked in 1888, bottomed in 1891 and was back at its earlier peak by 1894, much sooner than in Victoria. But he says Queensland’s bank advances did not recover their 1889 level until 1916.'
  ], src: ['rba90', 'adbDrury', 'merrett', 'anuNZLM', 'anuBurnsPhilp'], image: 'qnb' },
  { h: 'The 1891 shearers’ strike: how it links to the money', p: [
   'The links the sources show are about who owned and led, not about orders from a bank. The Queensland State Archives says that in late 1890, as the wool price fell, employers drastically cut wages and entitlements for shearers. Woodley says the Queensland Pastoral Employers’ Association formed at Barcaldine in April 1889, and that pastoralists met in Melbourne and Sydney in November and December 1890 to form the Pastoralists’ Union of Australia.',
   'Woodley says entrepreneurs with Queensland interests rarely feature in accounts of the strike. Frank Murphy, an owner of extensive Queensland sheep stations and the member for Barcoo, became president of the Queensland association. George Fairbairn the younger, the managing director of the United Mortgage and Agency Company, called a meeting of the Queensland delegates before the Melbourne conference. Thomas McIlwraith, whom Woodley calls a wealthy pastoralist and financier and a member of the association, was Treasurer in 1891. He told the unions that the pastoralists were ready to meet only if the bush workers first accepted “freedom of contract”.',
   'Woodley writes that by April at least nine thousand men were on strike. He also says Aboriginal shearers were active unionists by the early 1890s and took part in the strike. He cites the historian Jordan Humphreys for this.',
   'No source read for this page says a London bank or company ordered the wage cuts. The link is that the owners, finance company managers and employers’ leaders overlapped.'
  ], src: ['qsaStrike', 'woodley'], flag: 'Sources differ on how many strike leaders were jailed. The State Archives blog says thirteen organisers were arrested in March and sent to St Helena for three years, and also says 21 men boarded the boat on 26 May. Woodley says fourteen men were tried at Rockhampton in May 1891 and twelve were convicted and sentenced to three years. The Timeline entry for the strike in the main resource gives thirteen.' },
  { h: 'The Federation Drought and the companies', p: [
   'The National Museum of Australia says the drought of 1895 to 1903 was the worst in Australia’s history measured by stock losses, that banks foreclosed on some stations, and that large stations were divided into smaller holdings.',
   'Bowen Downs shows what this meant on one company’s runs. The Muttaburra local history says the station was badly overstocked after land resumptions. It says that by 1902, 276,244 sheep had died at Bowen Downs and 134,000 remained, and that at Mt Cornish 2,000 head of cattle remained out of 27,417, with 644 horses dead. It says the Scottish Australian Company sold Mt Cornish in 1904 to recover some of its losses.',
   'The same local history says the Labour Government increased pastoral rents by as much as 100 per cent in 1902.'
  ], src: ['nma', 'muttaburra'], flag: 'The 1902 rent claim in the local history does not match the Queensland State Archives account used elsewhere in this resource, which says the Land Act of 1902 was a Land Act for lessees. The local history has no footnotes, and its claim is not used anywhere else here. Please check it against the Act.' }
 ],
 checks: [
  { name: 'Scottish Australian Investment Company', verdict: 'Yes, from 1861 to 1862', text: 'It financed Bowen Downs, with first cattle in October 1862, and Wallis and Burke say its partners had stocked Beamesbrook in the Burke district by 1865. The four other stations in its Queensland archive, Mt Emu Plains, Kynuna, Albion Downs and Lucknow, joined its holdings only in the early 1950s, so they are not part of this story.', src: ['jcuBowen', 'jcuScot', 'atfMitchell', 'atfBurke', 'anuSAIC'] },
  { name: 'Union Bank of Australia', verdict: 'Yes, from 1858', text: 'It was founded in London in 1837 and opened a Brisbane branch in 1858. In 1864 parliament asked it to place the Queensland Government’s loan, and in 1866 the Government owed it nearly £500,000.', src: ['unionWiki', 'hodes1866'] },
  { name: 'Bank of New South Wales', verdict: 'Yes, from 1850. Not a London bank', text: 'It opened its Brisbane branch in November 1850 and was one of the four banks in Queensland by late 1862. It was based in Sydney, not London. No source read confirms the idea that it was a London bank.', src: ['slqBNSW', 'boq'] },
  { name: 'Bank of Australasia', verdict: 'Yes, from 1853. Pastoral lending not shown', text: 'It opened a branch at Ipswich on 7 November 1853 and was one of the four banks in Queensland by late 1862. No source read shows how much it lent to Queensland pastoralists.', src: ['qutBoA', 'boq'] },
  { name: 'Queensland National Bank', verdict: 'Yes, but set up in Brisbane, with British depositors', text: 'It was set up in Brisbane in 1872 by Queensland businessmen. It had a London branch by 1914, and Scottish depositors in 1893. It held the Government’s account from 1879 and suspended payment in May 1893. Pastoral stations ended up with the bank.', src: ['qldHeritageQNB', 'wikiQNB', 'rba90', 'eoasQNPC'] },
  { name: 'Dalgety and Company', verdict: 'Not in Queensland until 1891', text: 'Dalgety built its business in Victoria, New Zealand and Sydney. The company was registered in London in 1884, and its first Queensland branch opened at Rockhampton in 1891, then Brisbane in 1894. No source read shows a Dalgety branch in Queensland in the 1860s to 1880s.', src: ['adbDalgety', 'anuDalgety'] },
  { name: 'Goldsbrough Mort and Company', verdict: 'Partly shown: 1890', text: 'The company was formed in 1888. A catalogue entry lists a letter from its Rockhampton manager asking for further advances in June 1890. I could not confirm what Mort and Company or Goldsbrough did in Queensland before that.', src: ['anuGM'] },
  { name: 'Australian Mercantile Land and Finance Company', verdict: 'Brief in the 1860s; small until the 1900s', text: 'It was registered in England in 1863 and opened in Brisbane that year. It moved its business to Victoria in 1865. By 1904 it held 80 stations as security for loans, six of them in Queensland, and it bought its first Queensland station, Retro, in 1906.', src: ['anuAMLF', 'qhAMLF'] },
  { name: 'New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company', verdict: 'Yes, from 1884', text: 'It was registered in England in 1865 but opened in Brisbane and Rockhampton only in 1884. It suspended business in July 1893 and was reconstructed in 1894.', src: ['anuNZLM'] },
  { name: 'North British Australasian Company', verdict: 'Partly shown: before 1860', text: 'The only Queensland evidence read is a short excerpt of a biography. It says John Deuchar worked for the company from 1842 to 1855 and managed Goomburra and Rosenthal. I found nothing on its part in the 1860s to 1890s.', src: ['adbDeuchar'] },
  { name: 'Bank of Queensland (1863–1866)', verdict: 'Yes, a London-formed bank that failed in 1866', text: 'It was not on the original list, but the sources show it was formed in London, opened in Brisbane in August 1863 and suspended payment in 1866. Its London board said it had lost a large part of its capital on advances against stations and other securities.', src: ['boq'] },
  { name: 'United Mortgage and Agency Company of Australia', verdict: 'Yes, through the Fairbairn family, by 1890', text: 'Woodley names George Fairbairn the younger as its managing director and says the Fairbairns owned an interest in about one in five Queensland sheep. I did not find when the company began lending in Queensland.', src: ['woodley'] },
  { name: 'Other names', verdict: 'Not checked in depth', text: 'The Australian Joint Stock Bank was one of the four banks in Queensland by late 1862, according to Wikipedia. Gilchrist, Watt and Company, a Sydney firm, held John Douglas’s mortgage. I did not read enough about other firms to say more.', src: ['boq', 'hodesDouglas'] }
 ],
 means: [
  { h: 'For Aboriginal people', p: [
   'Woodley writes that by the 1880s graziers were moving into the Mitchell, Maranoa and Warrego districts, and that this was accomplished through extensive violence and disruption inflicted on Aboriginal people with the aid of a Native Mounted Police force directed by white officers. The district pages used for the map give earlier first-run dates for these districts, in the 1840s and 1860s. Wallis and Burke say Aboriginal resistance in the Mitchell district was defeated less than two decades after it opened for white occupation.',
   'On Bowen Downs, the station financed by the Scottish Australian Investment Company, Wallis and Burke name Alexander “Long” Gordon as the first manager. They say he was responsible for the mass killing of 13 Aboriginal people in mid-1866, in revenge for the killing of a white man some weeks earlier. They also say the Aramac manager of that name is thought to have massacred at least 13 Iningai-speaking people in mid-1866. Other sources name Nat Buchanan as the first manager, so the sources disagree on who managed the station. The sources read do not say what the company’s London or Scottish owners knew.',
   'Woodley also writes that Traditional Owners kept negotiating access to Country and kept working for the pastoral industry, but under exploitative conditions. Wallis and Burke say the station owner James Allan, in the Mitchell district, used Aboriginal labour on his stations. By the early 1890s many Aboriginal shearers were union members.',
   'When a run was mortgaged, the lender’s security was land that had been taken from its Traditional Owners. The sources read do not say whether the lenders knew or cared. That question is unanswered here.'
  ], src: ['woodley', 'atfMitchell', 'jcuBowen'] },
  { h: 'For workers', p: [
   'Woodley says machine shearing involved considerable investment by station owners and made conflict worse. The Queensland State Archives says wages and entitlements were cut in late 1890 as the wool price fell. Woodley says that as wool prices fell and the depression deepened in the years after the strike, bush workers’ bargaining power only weakened.',
   'Woodley also writes that, for some workers, excluding Chinese people and South Sea Islanders was a fundamental plank of their unionism, and that in places Chinese businesses were menaced during the strike. This is part of the record of who the workers were and what they asked for.'
  ], src: ['woodley', 'qsaStrike'] },
  { h: 'For selectors', p: [
   'The Queensland State Archives summary of the land laws says the Act of 1868 let selectors choose land in settled districts that was not under pastoral lease, and that the Act of 1884 abolished pre-emptive purchases by pastoralists. The existing Land laws pages of this resource cover the rest.',
   'I did not find a source that shows how banks and finance companies treated selectors as a group, or how many selectors lost their land through debt.'
  ], src: ['qsaLand'] }
 ],
 gaps: [
  'How many Queensland runs were held by London-linked lenders in any one year. No source read gives a Queensland count for the 1860s to 1880s.',
  'What Goldsbrough Mort, Mort and Company, and the North British Australasian Company did in Queensland before 1890.',
  'The pastoral lending of the Bank of Australasia and the Bank of New South Wales in Queensland.',
  'What investors in London or Scotland knew about how the land was taken, and what they thought about it.',
  'How lenders treated selectors, and how many selectors lost land through debt.',
  'A source that says a bank or company ordered the wage cuts of 1890 to 1891. None was found.',
  'Newspaper reports of 1866 and 1893. They could not be searched here, so the newspapers are read only as quoted by historians.'
 ]
};

