I.disagree = I.disagree.concat([
 'Money from London: Agra and Masterman’s Bank is said to have suspended payment on 6 June 1866 (Wikipedia) and to have been “involved in the failure” of Overend Gurney (Hodes). Hodes also says it telegraphed on 22 July 1866 that it could carry out its agreement.',
 'Money from London: Wikipedia’s page on the Bank of Queensland puts the collapse of Overend Gurney in July 1866. Its page on Agra and Masterman’s says 10 May 1866. This resource uses 10 May for London and 10 July for the news reaching Brisbane.',
 'Money from London: the Wikipedia page on the Bank of Queensland says the four banks in Queensland by late 1862 were “all from other Australian colonies”. The RBA calls the Bank of Australasia and the Union Bank English-based banks. This resource gives each bank’s own origin.',
 'Money from London: Merrett says the Queensland National Bank’s insolvency came out in 1896 after Drury’s death. The RBA says a government committee found it deeply insolvent in 1897.',
 'Money from London: sources differ on who was first manager of Bowen Downs (Nat Buchanan, in the James Cook University archive and Wikipedia, or Alexander “Long” Gordon, in Wallis and Burke) and on its early size and stock (3,000 cattle and 1,500 square miles in Wikipedia, 5,000 cattle in October 1862 and 3,683 square miles eventually in the Muttaburra local history).',
 'Money from London: the Muttaburra local history says the Labour Government raised pastoral rents by as much as 100 per cent in 1902. The Queensland State Archives account used elsewhere in this resource calls the Act of 1902 a Land Act for lessees. The rent claim is not used elsewhere.',
 'Money from London: sources differ on how many strike leaders were jailed in 1891. The State Archives blog says thirteen arrested in March and also 21 men boarding the boat on 26 May. Woodley says fourteen tried and twelve convicted in May. The Timeline in the main resource says thirteen sentenced on 20 May.',
 'Money from London: the Wikipedia page on Bowen Downs says blackbirded South Sea Islanders did much of the labour there in the 1870s and 1880s, and cites one 1876 newspaper item. The newspaper was not read, so this resource does not state it as a fact.',
 'Money from London: the figures for British investment (the £18 million, £28 million and £7 million) are for all of Australia, and come from a journal of the time quoted by Merrett. No Queensland-only figure was found.',
 'Money from London: pages from the Australian Dictionary of Biography, the Queensland Historical Atlas and several archive catalogues could not be fetched in full. Where that happened the source note says the page was read as a search-tool excerpt. Those facts need a check against the full page.'
]);

I.overview.sections.splice(5, 0, { h: 'Money from London', p: [
 'The runs were stocked and fenced with borrowed money, and some of it came from banks and companies in London and Scotland or from British savers. A wool lien let a squatter pay a lender from the next wool clip. The “Money from London” page names the lenders, checks how much each was really in Queensland, and follows the money through the financial crisis of 1866, the land boom of the 1880s, the bank failures of 1893 and the Federation Drought.',
 'The Bank of New South Wales opened the first bank in Brisbane in 1850, and the Union Bank followed in 1858. The Scottish Australian Investment Company financed Bowen Downs, which received its first cattle in 1862. Dalgety, in contrast, did not open in Queensland until 1891.'
], src: ['slqBNSW', 'unionWiki', 'jcuBowen', 'anuDalgety'] });
I.overview.glance.push({ n: '35.6% to 91.6%', label: 'bank advances in Queensland as a share of the colony’s output, 1880 and 1892', src: ['merrett'] });
I.overview.words = I.overview.words.concat([
 ['Lien', 'A claim a lender has on something, such as the next wool clip, so the lender is paid from it first.'],
 ['Stock mortgage', 'A loan in which animals are the security. The lender can claim them if the loan is not repaid.'],
 ['Mortgagee', 'The lender who holds a mortgage.'],
 ['Foreclose', 'To take property because a loan was not repaid.'],
 ['Debenture', 'A written promise to repay a loan with interest. Companies sold them to savers.'],
 ['Agency house', 'A firm that sold a squatter’s wool and supplied goods, and often lent money. It is also called a stock and station agent.'],
 ['Pastoral finance company', 'A company that lent money to pastoralists, often money it had collected from savers or borrowed from banks.'],
 ['Suspend payment', 'When a bank stops paying depositors on demand.'],
 ['Reconstruction', 'A plan to reopen a failed bank on new terms, with depositors waiting longer for their money.'],
 ['Gazetted', 'Announced officially in the Government Gazette. A district was gazetted when the Government named it. Settlers could arrive before or after that date.']
]);
I.description = I.description + ' It also covers the money from London and Scotland behind the runs, and a map of how settlement spread by decade.';

// ---------- folklore: one story with a source ----------
I.folklore.stories.push({
 title: 'The banker whose coffin held only stones',
 where: 'Brisbane · after 3 February 1896',
 text: [
  'The story says that Edward Drury, the general manager of the Queensland National Bank, did not really die in 1896. After his death some sensational claims said he had faked his death, and that his coffin held only stones.'
 ],
 from: 'The Australian Dictionary of Biography entry for Drury mentions the story.',
 known: [
  'What is documented: the Australian Dictionary of Biography says Drury was general manager of the bank from 1872 and died on 3 February 1896. Merrett and the RBA say that the bank was later found to be deeply insolvent, and the Government guaranteed its deposits in 1897.',
  'Why the story is doubtful: the entry says the claims were never proved. This resource found no other source for the story, and did not read the sensational journals. The sources do not say why the story spread.'
 ],
 src: ['adbDrury', 'merrett', 'rba90']
});
I.folklore.intro = I.folklore.intro.replace('collects five of them', 'collects six of them');
I.folkTeach = (I.folkTeach || []).concat([{
 q: 'A bank fails and its manager dies. People say he is alive abroad and that his coffin holds stones. What would a historian need to see before treating this as more than a rumour?',
 a: [
  'A death certificate, a burial record, a newspaper report of the funeral, or a statement by someone who saw the coffin would count as evidence. The source says the claims were never proved.',
  'Ask students why a rumour like this might spread when many depositors lost money. The source does not say, so treat any answer as a guess, and ask how a historian could test it.'
 ]
}]);

// ---------- teacher data (removed from the student build by build.js) ----------
I.teacherNotes = I.teacherNotes.concat([
 'Money from London: much of the evidence is from histories of all of Australia. Ask students to check each time whether a figure is for Queensland.',
 'Money from London: some pages in the source list could only be read as search-tool excerpts, and these are marked. Look at the full pages before you rely on those facts in class.',
 'Money from London: the Bowen Downs entry includes a documented mass killing in 1866. Read it first and decide how to introduce it. The sources disagree about who managed the station then.',
 'The map of settlement uses “first run” dates and “gazetted” dates, which are different things. Ask students to look at both.'
]);
I.discuss = I.discuss.concat([
 { q: 'Why did a squatter need to borrow money before he had sold any wool? Use the 1909 Government review and the Burke district page.', a: ['A run had to be stocked before the lease could be applied for, so stock, fences and wages were paid for first. One wool clip could be held on a remote station until the next shearing, so a lien on the clip let a lender be paid from it. The review says the interest, with the agent’s commission, was commonly 12 per cent a year.', 'Students should say that the review was written in 1909 and is favourable to the colony.'] },
 { q: 'Which companies on the “Who was really in Queensland?” list were in Queensland in the 1860s, and which were not? Why does it matter to check this?', a: ['In Queensland in the 1860s: the Scottish Australian Investment Company (Bowen Downs, 1862), the Union Bank (from 1858), the Bank of New South Wales (1850), the Bank of Australasia (1853) and the London-formed Bank of Queensland (1863). AML&F opened in 1863 and left in 1865. Dalgety did not open until 1891, and the New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company not until 1884.', 'It matters because a well-known name is not proof of a part in Queensland. A claim in an essay needs a source for the place and the date.'] },
 { q: 'Describe the 1866 crisis in your own words. Why did a failure in London stop public works in Queensland?', a: ['The Queensland Government depended on borrowing through banks and London agents. A London bank that was to place part of its loan was involved in the failure of Overend Gurney, and the Union Bank cancelled a promised advance and refused government cheques. The Government issued Treasury bills. Works stopped, there were 156 bankruptcies and immigration stopped.', 'Ask what the sources say about squatters. One squatter wrote that the panic hurt those who were largely in debt or whose credit was not good.'] },
 { q: 'John Douglas lost Tooloombah to the lender in 1867. What does his case show, and what can one case not show?', a: ['It shows how a mortgage could end with the lender taking the station, and that the sale price could be less than the debt.', 'One case cannot show how many squatters lost runs. The statement that “ninety-nine out of every hundred squatters” were insolvent was a member of parliament’s claim in a debate, not a count.'] },
 { q: 'The 1909 Government review says that from 1888 to 1893 values were held up as far as possible by the mortgagees. Why might lenders do this? What could go wrong?', a: ['The review says they believed the stagnation must be temporary. Lenders who admitted property was worth less would have had to accept that their loans were worth less.', 'If values stayed too high, bad loans were not cleared and the failures came later, in 1893. The sources do not give lenders’ own reasons, so students should say their answer is a suggestion.'] },
 { q: 'What does the evidence show about the link between the money and the 1891 shearers’ strike? What does it not show?', a: ['It shows that the employers’ leaders and the finance company managers overlapped, and that wages were cut as the wool price fell.', 'It does not show that a bank or London company ordered the cuts. No source read says so. Students should keep “link” and “cause” separate.'] },
 { q: 'The land taken from Traditional Owners became security for loans. What do the sources say about what the lenders knew? What is missing?', a: ['They do not say. The sources say the land was taken with extensive violence, that a mass killing of 13 Aboriginal people was linked to Bowen Downs in 1866, and that Aboriginal people kept working on stations under exploitative conditions. No source read gives a lender’s own words on this.', 'Students might suggest where to look, such as company letters in the Noel Butlin Archives, or Traditional Owners’ own accounts. Read the Bowen Downs entry yourself first.'] },
 { q: 'Choose two districts on the map. For each, give the year of the first run and the gazettal date. Why are they different, and which tells you more about when settlement began?', a: ['Examples: the Darling Downs (first runs 1840, district formally opened 1843) and Port Curtis (first runs 1853, proclaimed 10 January 1854). A gazettal is the Government’s announcement. Runs were often taken up before it, as in the southern Leichhardt.', 'The first run date tells more about when people arrived. The gazettal date tells more about when the Government began to manage the district. Sources also disagree on some dates, and the map says so.'] }
]);
